---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="The Vision" description="A semi-autonomous community in the Bolivian cloud forest. Five components, one system." heroImage="/images/yungas/coroico-clouds.jpg">
  <!-- Photo Hero -->
  <section class="relative min-h-[50vh] flex items-end pb-16 overflow-hidden">
    <img src="/images/yungas/coroico-clouds.jpg" alt="Coroico in morning cloud" width="1600" height="1068" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/80 via-black/40 to-black/20"></div>
    <div class="relative z-10 max-w-[1400px] mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 w-full">
      <span class="text-label text-accent tracking-widest reveal">Vision</span>
      <h1 class="text-headline text-white mt-4 reveal" data-delay="100">The Vision</h1>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-white/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl reveal" data-delay="200">A community in the Bolivian cloud forest, learning to provision its own power, food, shelter, and governance -- and to keep doing so when the external systems it once depended on stop being reliable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Content with sidebar TOC -->
  <section class="pb-32 px-6 lg:px-10 pt-16">
    <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto lg:max-w-[1100px] lg:flex lg:gap-16">

      <!-- Sidebar TOC (desktop only) -->
      <aside class="hidden lg:block lg:w-[200px] lg:shrink-0 lg:order-2">
        <div class="sticky top-24" id="toc-container">
          <p class="text-label text-bark-400 mb-4">On this page</p>
          <nav id="toc-nav" class="space-y-1.5" aria-label="Table of contents">
            <!-- Populated by JS from h2 elements -->
          </nav>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <!-- Main content -->
      <div class="content-prose lg:flex-1 lg:min-w-0 lg:order-1">
        <p>
          BARK -- the Bolivian Ark -- is taking root at 1,750 meters in the Yungas, the subtropical crease where
          the Andes collapse into the Amazon basin. The name matters. An ark is not a bunker. It carries living
          things through rough water and sets them down somewhere they can keep living. Bunkers entomb.
          Arks travel.
        </p>

        <h2>A Community, Not a Compound</h2>
        <div class="content-two-col">
          <p>
            Compounds store supplies behind walls. Communities produce what they need. BARK runs on five linked components:
            a <strong>fabrication lab and techno park</strong> where broken water pumps get repaired, circuit boards
            get milled, and appropriate technology gets documented in the open; a <strong>startup incubator</strong>
            that launches ventures rooted in Coroico's actual soil and actual needs; a <strong>trading house</strong>
            that moves Yungas coffee, cacao, and craft goods through channels the growers built themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>retreat center</strong> where Aymara healers and biosensor engineers sit in the same room,
            comparing notes on what the body is doing during ceremony; and the <strong>infrastructure layer</strong>
            underneath -- solar panels, spring-fed cisterns, vertical growing walls, mesh radios -- that makes
            autonomy a material fact, not an aspiration on a slide deck.
          </p>
        </div>
        <blockquote><p>A maker space without its own power grid is a hobby. A retreat center that cannot feed its guests is a resort with incense.</p></blockquote>

        <p>
          No single component here is unprecedented. Solar works. Permaculture works. Cooperatives work.
          The ambition is in the connections between them -- shared power, shared data, shared labor pools
          that let each component compensate when another struggles. When the road to La Paz washes out in
          the February rains, the community keeps eating, keeps communicating, keeps working.
        </p>

        <div class="photo-frame h-64 md:h-96 my-12 reveal"><img src="/images/yungas/valley.jpg" alt="Yungas valley, Bolivia" width="1920" height="1282" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><div class="photo-credit">CC BY-SA 3.0 via Wikimedia Commons</div></div>

        <h2>Open Source as Survival Strategy</h2>
        <p>
          Every solar array schematic, every governance vote, every agricultural trial result gets published.
          This is practical, not altruistic. Open-source projects attract better collaborators,
          survive their founders, and resist capture by any single faction. If BARK works, the plans should
          spread. One community in one valley is an experiment. Twenty communities
          across three continents is a proof of concept.
        </p>

        <h2>Embedded, Not Parachuted In</h2>
        <p>
          Coroico has existed since before the Spanish arrived. The terraced slopes above town carry a thousand
          years of agricultural intelligence in their contour lines. This place does not need rescue. What it
          can use is investment that stays put, jobs that persist beyond a construction phase, and technology
          transfer that runs in both directions -- the fabrication lab learns from the farmer as much as the
          farmer learns from the lab. BARK hires locally, buys locally, and submits its governance to mechanisms
          that give the Coroiqueno community real leverage over what happens on their land.
        </p>

        <h2>Capability, Not Stockpiles</h2>
        <p>
          Hoarding is a losing game. Supplies run out. Canned food expires. Skills atrophy in storage. BARK invests
          in living capability instead. The question is never "how many months of rice do we have in the warehouse"
          but "can we grow cassava, mill a replacement pump shaft, stitch a wound, and teach someone else to do all
          three?" Energy independence, local manufacturing, medical knowledge preserved in daily practice rather
          than entombed on a hard drive. A community that can repair its own systems does not need to predict
          which specific crisis arrives next.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-box">
          <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">The first cohort is forming.</p>
          <p>We need engineers, capital, and people who finish things. <a href="/en/participate">What the work looks like</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  function initContentPage() {
    const content = document.querySelector('.content-prose');
    const tocNav = document.getElementById('toc-nav');
    if (!content || !tocNav) return;

    const headings = content.querySelectorAll('h2');
    if (headings.length < 2) {
      const tocContainer = document.getElementById('toc-container');
      if (tocContainer) tocContainer.style.display = 'none';
      return;
    }

    while (tocNav.firstChild) {
      tocNav.removeChild(tocNav.firstChild);
    }

    headings.forEach((h2) => {
      if (!h2.id) {
        h2.id = 'section-' + (h2.textContent || '').trim().toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-').replace(/(^-|-$)/g, '');
      }
      const link = document.createElement('a');
      link.href = '#' + h2.id;
      link.textContent = h2.textContent || '';
      tocNav.appendChild(link);
    });

    const tocLinks = tocNav.querySelectorAll('a');
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      entries.forEach(entry => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          const id = entry.target.id;
          tocLinks.forEach(link => {
            link.classList.toggle('toc-active', link.getAttribute('href') === '#' + id);
          });
        }
      });
    }, { rootMargin: '-80px 0px -70% 0px', threshold: 0 });

    headings.forEach(h2 => observer.observe(h2));
  }

  initContentPage();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initContentPage);
</script>
